viewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2041009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"005ba59a845bf413642ad8be9083d8f1e0abaffe75942fb9a1751fe31233fbb1","compact":"cd507c8d772a28998da41b916be1583834954bed1852167c99da27d3e4c30a4c","nasal":"bd39a3a6e7b26d657f0a8249f2a3d3f3e87a77a1d201990451c6f8b9684c2a4d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-41-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":41,"verse":10,"english":"Having said this to Sumitrā's son, Raghava spoke to Sumantra, saying, “O mild one, do you now heedfully tend the horses.” Then at sunset, fastening the horses, Sumantra fed them plentifully with grass, and then came back.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.882,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-46-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2041010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f260ddeb376a2f767b56b4356a360d4b978b3c4d2e2c5f4990bba2a6f4b4b35f","compact":"baaedeaf4cf47a29de263136801e6d299aba35d7ebbd7a949c5c7911f147ed6b","nasal":"2d81fdb46824633e3fb2e02359ff4f44b9effe174f7638548121a5ab5194516c"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-41-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":41,"verse":11,"english":"Then seeing the night arrived, the charioteer worshipped the beneficent Sandhyā, and then in company with Sumitrā's son, prepared Rāma's bed. on water","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.841,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-46-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2041011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"086e0387aff09db68586c3dbdab96b6589461f30b6ec1f8dbd16d3dc5c527cb9","compact":"4697d4728f80b21934dafa289358668d28808f1d2ededf29b1224502ff0ab279","nasal":"4697d4728f80b21934dafa289358668d28808f1d2ededf29b1224502ff0ab279"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-41-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":41,"verse":12,"english":"And looking at that bed on the shores of the Tamasā surrounded by trees, Rāma along with his wife and the son of Sumitrā, lay down,","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.75,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-46-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2041012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4e6c522330f242553da8b7011e3954238230d3f5ef81b7327c6a333f1c3d05d8","compact":"02b2079831382e5e6885abf584b2eedf8b807997d5e6c5f95a79a8107eb9e670","nasal":"02b2079831382e5e6885abf584b2eedf8b807997d5e6c5f95a79a8107eb9e670"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-41-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":41,"verse":13,"english":"When Lakşmaņa found that Rāma afflicted with fatigue had slept together with his spouse, he began to speak to the charioteer concerning the various qualities of Rāma.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.627,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-46-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2041013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c99eef08b5528e4807d3e6ff671964b1043d01b3dcd3b5831c4d438cfee88b2f","compact":"627c115ac16163ed3c0e10997b415ac9ac00ee3b6c2ed215d874eb14dcbcce65","nasal":"627c115ac16163ed3c0e10997b415ac9ac00ee3b6c2ed215d874eb14dcbcce65"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-41-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":41,"verse":14,"english":"As remaining awake in the night, Sumitrā's son was engaged in expatiating to the charioteer on the virtues of Rāma on the banks of the Tamasă, the sun arose.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.727,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-46-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2041014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bc2a05802928e911f857a4323aac0ce60cdfc4be3f62ecbffa7c9475ffb1d589","compact":"72a7bf2c87ba8f13aac8b8b78627dc143fdcd1ad66fba730c9c91f1da4bf244f","nasal":"72a7bf2c87ba